## sync.WaitGroup

`sync.WaitGroup` is the idiomatic way to **wait for a collection of goroutines to finish**. It's better than `time.Sleep` because it's deterministic.

## Three Main Methods

| Method | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `wg.Add(n)` | Add `n` to the counter |
| `wg.Done()` | Decrement counter by 1 |
| `wg.Wait()` | Block until counter reaches 0 |

## Basic Example

```go
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"sync"
	"time"
)

func worker(id int, wg *sync.WaitGroup) {
	defer wg.Done() // ensure Done is always called

	fmt.Printf("Worker %d starting\n", id)
	time.Sleep(time.Second)
	fmt.Printf("Worker %d done\n", id)
}

func main() {
	var wg sync.WaitGroup

	for i := 1; i <= 5; i++ {
		wg.Add(1) // increment before go statement
		go worker(i, &wg)
	}

	wg.Wait() // wait for all workers to finish
	fmt.Println("All workers completed")
}
```

## Fan-Out Pattern

Fan-out: distribute one task across many goroutines:

```go
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"sync"
)

func processItem(item int, results chan<- int, wg *sync.WaitGroup) {
	defer wg.Done()
	results <- item * item // square
}

func main() {
	items := []int{1, 2, 3, 4, 5}
	results := make(chan int, len(items))
	var wg sync.WaitGroup

	// Fan-out: spawn a goroutine per item
	for _, item := range items {
		wg.Add(1)
		go processItem(item, results, &wg)
	}

	// Close results channel after all finish
	go func() {
		wg.Wait()
		close(results)
	}()

	// Collect results
	for r := range results {
		fmt.Println(r)
	}
}
```

## Important Rules

1. **Always `Add` before `go`** — don't call `Add` inside the new goroutine
2. **Use `defer wg.Done()`** — ensures Done is called even on panic
3. **Pass `*sync.WaitGroup`** — always pass a pointer, never a value

